\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.1}{\ignorespaces The transimpedance amplifier used to measure the dark noise of a variety of photodiodes. For large feedback resistors (R2 in the circuit above), a JFET style OP amp such as the TL071 performs better since these have low current noise (e.g. see \cite  {AD797} for a description of noise sources in OP amp circuits). In this application, the TL071 is optimised for between 100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\SIunits {\ensuremath  {\SI@fstyle  {k}}\ensuremath  {\SI@fstyle  {\ensuremath  {\Omega }}}} to 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\SIunits {\ensuremath  {\SI@fstyle  {M}}\ensuremath  {\SI@fstyle  {\ensuremath  {\Omega }}}}.\relax }}{33}}
\newlabel{fig:tl071}{{8.1}{33}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{33}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {8.11}Experimental Results from Testing Extended InGaAs Photodiodes.}{33}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{33}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.2}{\ignorespaces Transimpedance amplifier used to measure the quantum efficiency and linearity of photodiodes for up to 20\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mW of 1550\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}nm laser light. The reverse bias supplied to the photodiode is provided by the first OP amp (OP27). The two inputs $\mathrm  {V_{bias}}$ and $\mathrm  {V_{mod}}$ allows for a quiet low passed DC bias to be set while also being able to perform lock-in type measurements.\relax }}{34}}
\newlabel{fig:ad797pdtester}{{8.2}{34}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.3}{\ignorespaces Layout of experiment. PD\textsubscript  {IL} is the in-loop photodiode (C30641gh) and PD\textsubscript  {OOL} is the photodiode being tested. The PPCL300 is a 1550nm fibre laser from pure photonics. Since the C30641gh is a well-characterised diode, this was used as the in-loop diode for the amplitude stabilisation\relax }}{34}}
\newlabel{fig:testsetup}{{8.3}{34}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.4}{\ignorespaces The dark noise of the FD10D at different reverse biases. The maximum datasheet value is 1.8V, above this voltage electrons can start tunnelling across the band gap. The rolling off above \SIunits {9}{\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}kHz} is an artefact of the circuit. Above 0.65\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V reverse bias, the $1/f$ noise becomes larger than shot noise. At this reverse bias, the quantum efficiency is $\sim $70\%. These measurements were made at room temperature.\relax }}{35}}
\newlabel{fig:fd10ddarknoisereversebias}{{8.4}{35}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{35}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.5}{\ignorespaces The dark noise of the FD10D with a reverse bias of 1.8V at room temperature and 14\SIunits {\ensuremath  {\SI@fstyle  {\no@qsk  \ensuremath  {^{\circ }}}}C}. Although there is a slight reduction in noise, this comes at the expense of less quantum efficiency near the cut off wavelength.\relax }}{36}}
\newlabel{fig:fd10ddarknoisetemp18v}{{8.5}{36}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.6}{\ignorespaces A 1550\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}nm laser was used to illuminate the FD10D at room temperature. As the reverse bias is increased, the photo-current out of the FD10D increases by about 17\%. This increase in photo-current is due to an increase quantum efficiency since the noise of photo-current matched shot noise.\relax }}{37}}
\newlabel{fig:dc11mw436um}{{8.6}{37}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{37}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{37}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.7}{\ignorespaces Low noise fixed bias voltage dark noise measurement circuit. The 10V reference is generated by an AD587, in this circuit the noise is limited by the voltage noise of the second OP27, but it still reaches 5\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}$\text  {nV}_{\mathrm  {rms}}/\sqrt  {\text  {Hz}}$ at \SIunits {10}{\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz} which is 10 times smaller than the amount of voltage noise which could be converted to current noise that would cause the signal at the output of the TL071 not to be limited by the current noise of the TL071, given the typical dynamic impedance for extended InGaAs photodiodes.\relax }}{38}}
\newlabel{fig:darknoisetestbox2}{{8.7}{38}}
\newlabel{model}{{8.2}{38}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {8.12}Conclusion}{38}}
\newlabel{fig:cross_check}{{8.8a}{39}}
\newlabel{sub@fig:cross_check}{{a}{39}}
\newlabel{fig:FD10D_dark_noise_1V_noise_sources}{{8.8b}{39}}
\newlabel{sub@fig:FD10D_dark_noise_1V_noise_sources}{{b}{39}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.8}{\ignorespaces The left panel shows that the quiet reverse bias voltage source, shown in figure 8.7\hbox {}, and the more noisy but more flexible bias voltage source, shown in figure 8.1\hbox {}, gave the same results when the dark noise of the FD10D was measured. This means that the flexible reverse bias source is suitable to use for measuring the dark noise of other photodiodes. The bias voltage noise in the quiet bias voltage circuit was found to meet the requirements as shown in the right panel.\relax }}{39}}
\newlabel{fig:bias_checks}{{8.8}{39}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {8.13}Acknowledgements}{39}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.9}{\ignorespaces The dark noise of two different extended InGaAs photodiodes from Laser Components measured at room temperature. The noise curve for the IG24x500S4i at 0.004V is limited by the noise of the circuit shown in Figure 8.1\hbox {}. As the reverse bias is reduced, so is the $1/f$ noise. The IG26x500S4i had to be used at a lower reverse bias than the IG24x500S4i to achieve the same noise performance.\relax }}{40}}
\newlabel{fig:lasercomponentsnoise}{{8.9}{40}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.10}{\ignorespaces Using the apparatus shown in figure 8.3\hbox {}, the DC photo-current that the Laser Components extended InGaAs photodiodes could be operated with while still being linear (i.e. the AC component of the signal would scale linearly with the DC photo-current) was investigated. The spot size on the photodiodes was made as large as possible while still containing 99\% of the light power on the photodiode, and were operated with the maximum reverse bias voltage that would still give $1/f$ noise less than 10mW shot noise at the frequencies of interest for the a gravitational wave detector. The laser's amplitude was modulated. Using a lock-in technique the AC response was measured and divided by the DC photo-current. This was compared to the AC/DC response of the in-loop regular InGaAs photodiode which behaved linearly. When the AC/DC ratio starts to become smaller, the photodiode is no longer behaving linearly. As these diodes become more extended, they perform more poorly.\relax }}{41}}
\newlabel{fig:ratio}{{8.10}{41}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.11}{\ignorespaces The forward IV curve of the IG24x500S4i.\relax }}{42}}
\newlabel{fig:forwardbiasiv}{{8.11}{42}}
